\begin_layout Title
Class Notes: 01/30/14
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Price Discrimination
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Is there a way firms can charge different prices to different prices in
 order to extract all the surplus (increase welfare) possible and increase
 their profits?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The primary condition is that there is no arbitrage (resale)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Dichotomy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
First-Degree
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Downward sloping demand
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The monopolist must discern each buyer's willingness to pay.
 Then, for each buyer, the monopolist charges a price equal to marginal
 cost plus a unique fixed fee minus epsilon, based on their willingness
 to pay.
 This extracts all the possible surplus possible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Second-Degree
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Heterogeneous consumers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you knew each consumer's type, pick a blunde 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,q)$
\end_inset

 for each type and extract all the surplus.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But if monopolist does not know individual types, it must ensure that each
 type buys their bundle.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Third-Degree
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Use observable signal to discern the customer's type.
 Then, perform the basic pricing decision design for each type
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Optimal pricing for each group: 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{p_{i}^{*}-c}{p_{i}^{*}}=\frac{1}{\left|\eta_{D_{i}}\right|}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Second-Degree Price Discrimination
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose we have a monopolist that faces consumers of unknown type
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{1}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $Pr=\lambda$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{2}=1-\lambda$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 product at unit 
\begin_inset Formula $c<\theta_{1}<\theta_{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}=\begin{cases}
\theta_{i}V(q_{i})-T_{i} & \mbox{if pay \ensuremath{T_{i}} and consumer \ensuremath{q_{i}}}\\
0 & \mbox{if don't buy}
\end{cases}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $V(0)=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{\prime}>0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{\prime\prime}<0$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{2}>\theta_{1}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The monopolist choose 2 bundles 
\begin_inset Formula $(q_{1},T_{1})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(q_{2},T_{2})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Optimization problem for the monopolist:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{(q_{1},T_{1}),(q_{2},T_{2})}} & \lambda\left[T_{1}-cq_{1}\right]+\left(1-\lambda\right)\left[T_{2}-cq_{2}\right]\\
\mbox{s.t.} & \theta_{2}V(q_{2})-T_{2}\geq0 & (IR)_{H}\\
 & \theta_{1}V(q_{1})-T_{1}\geq0 & (IR)_{L}\\
 & \theta_{2}V(q_{2})-T_{2}\geq\theta_{2}V(q_{1})-T_{1} & (IC)_{H}\\
 & \theta_{1}V(q_{1})-T_{1}\geq\theta_{1}V(q_{2})T_{2} & (IC)_{L}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is important to note that the problem must satisfy the Individual Rationality
 (IR) constaint, and the Incentive Compatibililty (IC) constraint for each
 consumer.
 These two constraints come from mechanism design.
 The two constraints that are going to bind will be:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(IR)_{L}\implies T_{1}=\theta_{1}V(q_{1})
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
(IC)_{H}\implies\theta_{2}V(q_{2})-T_{2}=\theta_{2}V(q_{1})-T_{1}\\
\iff T_{2}=\theta_{2}V(q_{2})-\left(\theta_{2}-\theta_{1}\right)V(q_{1})
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\theta_{2}-\theta_{1}\right)V(q_{1})$
\end_inset

 is know as information rents.
 This is the amount of rents that must be paid by the monopolist in order
 for the high type to reveal that they are in fact a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

high type.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Now, the optimization problem becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{q_{1},q_{2}}\ \lambda\left[\theta_{1}V(q_{1})-cq_{1}\right]+(1-\lambda)\left[\theta_{2}V(q_{2})-(\theta_{2}-\theta_{1})V(q_{1})-cq_{2}\right]}
\]

\end_inset

We have the following F.O.C.:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\mbox{wrt}\ q_{2}:(1-\lambda)\left[\theta_{2}V^{\prime}(q_{2})-c\right]=0\ \mbox{(MU=MC)}\\
\mbox{wrt}\ q_{1}:\lambda\left[\theta_{1}V^{\prime}(q_{1})-c\right]+(1-\lambda)\left[(\theta_{1}-\theta_{2})V^{\prime}(q_{1})\right]=0\ (MU>MC)
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

So, the high types are efficiently consuming, while the low types are under
 consuming.
\end_layout

